class.description:
Point [member player] at a [SubtitlePlayer] and the label shows the segments that are visible now, one line each, in the order they started. The text of each line is [method SubtitleServer.get_display_text]: translated through the subtitles channel when [member Subtitle.auto_translate] is enabled, and decorated by [member SubtitleSegment.kind] ([code][Applause][/code], music notes, italics for off-screen and narration). Dialog lines get the speaker's name in front ([member show_speaker_names]) and the speaker's color ([member speaker_colors]).
With [member follow_accessibility_settings], the label shows nothing while [member AccessibilitySettings.subtitles_enabled] is off, drops closed captions while [member AccessibilitySettings.captions_enabled] is off, and its text size is [member font_size] scaled by [member AccessibilitySettings.subtitle_scale] and [member AccessibilitySettings.font_size_factor] (see [method AccessibilitySettingsServer.scale_subtitle_font_size]). [member SubtitleServer.captions_enabled] set to [code]false[/code] drops closed captions (sounds, reactions, music, lyrics), and [member SubtitleServer.enabled] set to [code]false[/code] hides all lines. The label updates at once when any of these change, and when the locale of the [code]subtitles[/code] channel changes ([method TranslationServer.set_locale_for]). The lines are translated only in the [code]subtitles[/code] domain: [member Node.auto_translate_mode] is [constant Node.AUTO_TRANSLATE_MODE_DISABLED] and read-only.
Speaker names are translated like the texts when [member Subtitle.auto_translate] is enabled ([method SubtitleServer.get_display_speaker]): in the domain [code]subtitles[/code] with the translation context [code]speaker[/code], which [method Subtitle.get_translatable_strings] also writes to the POT file.
In the editor, the label shows a sample line ([member preview_text], [member preview_speaker]) while no real line is visible, to place and style it.
The label writes [member RichTextLabel.text] itself: the text is not saved with the scene and is read-only in the Inspector. [member speaker_colors] is read when it is assigned: assign a new dictionary instead of changing the returned one, or call [method refresh]. While the game runs, the label hides itself while no line is shown, so a background [StyleBox] (theme item [code]normal[/code]) disappears with the text; to hide subtitles yourself, hide a parent node. Style it like any [RichTextLabel] (fonts, outline, shadow, background). [member SubtitleSegment.placement] is not applied: place the label where you want subtitles.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
func _ready():
    var label := SubtitleLabel.new()
    add_child(label)
    label.player = label.get_path_to($SubtitlePlayer)
    label.speaker_colors = { &"Anna": Color.GOLD, &"Ben": Color.SKY_BLUE }
    # Full width, 48 px above the bottom, growing upwards: long lines wrap and stay centered.
    label.set_anchors_and_offsets_preset(Control.PRESET_BOTTOM_WIDE, Control.PRESET_MODE_MINSIZE, 48)
    label.grow_vertical = Control.GROW_DIRECTION_BEGIN
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
public override void _Ready()
{
    var label = new SubtitleLabel();
    AddChild(label);
    label.Player = label.GetPathTo(GetNode("SubtitlePlayer"));
    label.SpeakerColors = new Godot.Collections.Dictionary<StringName, Color> { { "Anna", Colors.Gold }, { "Ben", Colors.SkyBlue } };
    // Full width, 48 px above the bottom, growing upwards: long lines wrap and stay centered.
    label.SetAnchorsAndOffsetsPreset(Control.LayoutPreset.BottomWide, Control.LayoutPresetMode.Minsize, 48);
    label.GrowVertical = Control.GrowDirection.Begin;
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
